>
>Just a note:
>     While the Saberhagan Swords trilogy is fantastic, the 
>Swords were made to provide interplay between powerful, 
>intelligent factions, often with armies and Gods backing 
>them.  I don't think they'd fit in well in a game where 
>there's only one player with any sort of guiding 
>intelligence, and everyone in the game except the Wizard 
>is intended to die rather quickly once encountered.  

Compare this, on the other hand, to Stormbringer and Excalibur, 
which were only used for killing off killer bees and grid bugs 
in the original versions.  :-)

>In addition, many of the Swords required incredible 
>sacrifice, which simply couldn't be born by one player.  

Most of this is actually present in the rules I gave ... for example,
Farslayer carries a similar risk in the game as in RL.  Some of it doesn't
carry over, true.

Also admitted, the Nethack player is nowhere near as likely to be involved
in combat with a monster with another Sword.

>Some of them weren't like that-- Townsaver, Stonecutter, 
>for example, but they weren't especially miraculous 
>either and aren't likely to add much to the game.

Townsaver doesn't work too well, true.  Stonecutter *I* would want.
A great sword and an even better pickaxe!  Plus, they should all be 
rustproof.

>     While they make great fantasy books, I'm not sure if 
>they could be used well in Nethack.  Too macroscopically 
>oriented.

Again, Stormbringer also was rather macroscopically oriented.  Elric 
slew armies with this thing; that doesn't happen all too often in the 
game.  But the ideas of the Swords, properly scaled (mostly as I suggested 
in the original message) would make interesting artifacts.  I think.

Gives people more trouble making wishes, too ... would you choose Stormy 
or Shieldbreaker for a dungeon like this?  Or maybe Woundhealer?

